{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MieKX3bbi2SPgXGM9AghBM","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL Acceptance Rates and Submission Statistics\n\nBased on the Paper Copilot statistics page, here are the key metrics:\n\n## Submissions and Acceptances by Year\n\n**CORL 2025** (Seoul, Korea)\n- Total submissions: 263\n- Accepted: 263 (100.00%)\n- Note: Appears to be preliminary/incomplete data\n\n**CORL 2024** (Munich, Germany)\n- Total submissions: 671\n- Accepted: 264 (39.34%)\n- Acceptance rate: approximately 39%\n\n**CORL 2023** (Atlanta, USA)\n- Total submissions: 498\n- Accepted: 199 (39.96%)\n- Breakdown: 166 posters (33.33%), 33 oral presentations (6.63%)\n- Acceptance rate: approximately 40%\n\n**CORL 2022**\n- Total submissions: 504\n- Accepted: 197 (39.09%)\n- Breakdown: 164 posters (32.54%), 33 oral (6.55%)\n\n**CORL 2021**\n- Total submissions: 408\n- Accepted: 153 (37.50%)\n- Breakdown: 127 posters (31.13%), 26 oral (6.37%)\n\n## Summary\n\nCoRL maintains a consistent acceptance rate of approximately **37-40%** across 2021-2024, with oral presentation rates around 6-7% of total submissions."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"31fb0b7f-0c9b-4a80-b3d9-4c267606ceee","uuid":"2b1572a0-0797-42b1-ba2e-8f5a6758a8c3","timestamp":"2026-05-23T04:43:24.526Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":143480,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL Acceptance Rates and Submission Statistics\n\nBased on the Paper Copilot statistics page, here are the key metrics:\n\n## Submissions and Acceptances by Year\n\n**CORL 2025** (Seoul, Korea)\n- Total submissions: 263\n- Accepted: 263 (100.00%)\n- Note: Appears to be preliminary/incomplete data\n\n**CORL 2024** (Munich, Germany)\n- Total submissions: 671\n- Accepted: 264 (39.34%)\n- Acceptance rate: approximately 39%\n\n**CORL 2023** (Atlanta, USA)\n- Total submissions: 498\n- Accepted: 199 (39.96%)\n- Breakdown: 166 posters (33.33%), 33 oral presentations (6.63%)\n- Acceptance rate: approximately 40%\n\n**CORL 2022**\n- Total submissions: 504\n- Accepted: 197 (39.09%)\n- Breakdown: 164 posters (32.54%), 33 oral (6.55%)\n\n**CORL 2021**\n- Total submissions: 408\n- Accepted: 153 (37.50%)\n- Breakdown: 127 posters (31.13%), 26 oral (6.37%)\n\n## Summary\n\nCoRL maintains a consistent acceptance rate of approximately **37-40%** across 2021-2024, with oral presentation rates around 6-7% of total submissions.","durationMs":3804,"url":"https://papercopilot.com/statistics/corl-statistics/"}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01P9byygWcXgPKi8GHgJNTx4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the CoRL review information:\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (OpenReview default, which CoRL uses)\n- Acceptance rate: ~39-40%\n- Review dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- Scale: 10=Top 5% seminal, 9=Top 15% strong accept, 8=Top 50% clear accept, 7=Good accept, 6=Marginally above, 5=Marginally below, 4=Not good enough, 3=Clear rejection, 2=Strong rejection, 1=Trivial/wrong\n\n\nI'll set up two reviewers to evaluate different aspects in parallel—one focusing on the technical methods and experimental validation, the other on novelty and how the work fits into the existing literature—then bring them together for a discussion.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":467,"cache_read_input_tokens":65694,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":467},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"31fb0b7f-0c9b-4a80-b3d9-4c267606ceee","uuid":"8a82abdb-b187-4aac-b62e-34c9c5e5d7a8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01P9byygWcXgPKi8GHgJNTx4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"CoRL acceptance rate is ~39-40%, using a 1-10 scale on OpenReview. Now dispatching two reviewers in parallel: R1 (methods + experiments) and R2 (novelty + related work)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":467,"cache_read_input_tokens":65694,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":467},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"31fb0b7f-0c9b-4a80-b3d9-4c267606ceee","uuid":"ef9d53f8-dc46-4dd8-85ee-9235dcfe5e0f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01P9byygWcXgPKi8GHgJNTx4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015yxwPeo6UdrjD9GQxcLuSz","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. \n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n- Acceptance rate: ~39-40%\n- Rating scale (OpenReview): 1-10 (10=Top 5% seminal, 9=Top 15% strong accept, 8=Top 50% clear accept, 7=Good accept, 6=Marginally above threshold, 5=Marginally below, 4=Not good enough, 3=Clear rejection, 2=Strong rejection, 1=Trivial/wrong)\n- Review dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n\n## Your focus areas\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the experimental design rigorous? Are controls appropriate? Are confounds addressed?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Sufficient seeds? Proper error bars? Valid significance claims?\n3. **Experiment coverage**: Are the experiments sufficient to support claims? What's missing?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Enough detail to replicate?\n\n## Paper summary\n\nTitle: \"Hidden Pitfalls of Auxiliary Heads in VLA Fine-tuning: A Cautionary Study on BatchNorm, Gradient Flow, and Safety\"\n\nThe paper studies whether auxiliary spatial supervision (BEV occupancy prediction) improves safety in VLA fine-tuning. Using OpenVLA (7.65B params) fine-tuned with LoRA on SafeLIBERO, they test 5 conditions: Full OCC, Random OCC, Detach, Depth, Vanilla. Key findings:\n- Detach+BN degrades CAR by -3.4pp vs Vanilla\n- Replacing BN with LN in detached head eliminates degradation (Detach+LN CAR=0.782 ≈ Vanilla=0.780)\n- CKA analysis: all conditions highly similar (>0.93)\n- Gradient cosine ≈ 0 between action and aux gradients\n- Dose-response (cutoff at 15K/25K/35K/45K): U-shaped, non-monotonic\n- 2x2 temporal design: only early-stop harms, consistent with BN mismatch\n- Conclusion: Detach harm is entirely BN artifact, not gradient flow; spatial aux supervision provides no safety benefit\n\nKey experimental details:\n- 3 seeds for main conditions (42, 43, 44), 4 seeds for Depth\n- Single seed for cutoff variants and Detach+LN\n- 500 episodes per seed (50 per task × 10 tasks)\n- Metrics: TSR, CAR, Collisions\n- OccHead: Linear(2176→256) + reshape + 2× ConvTranspose2d with BN2d + GELU, 1.2M params\n- Loss weight: w(t) = 0.01 × exp(-t/50000)\n- Cutoff@35K seed 44 shows partial collapse (predicted, not actual)\n\n## Experiment context from design document\nThe review_context.yaml reveals:\n- Cutoff@35K seed 44 (s2) results are **predicted, not actually run** — only s0 and s1 are actual\n- All cutoff conditions except @35K use single seeds\n- Detach+LN is single seed\n- Second benchmark (LIBERO-Object/Goal) was NOT run (P2 priority, 78 GPU hours)\n- The narrative_guidance says: \"Core 'gradient flow necessity' claim dissolved by BatchNorm confound. CKA and gradient cosine show no mechanism signal. Cutoff s2 collapse causes huge variance.\"\n- Plan_027 (Detach+LN) is described as the \"fatal experiment\" — single seed only\n\n## Your task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n2. Strengths (bulleted)\n3. Weaknesses (bulleted, detailed)\n4. Questions for authors\n5. Missing experiments\n6. Minor issues\n7. Scores for: Soundness, Empirical Rigor, Reproducibility, and an Overall Score (1-10) with justification\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Flag statistical weaknesses precisely. Consider whether claims are well-supported by the evidence shown."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":467,"cache_read_input_tokens":65694,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":467},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"31fb0b7f-0c9b-4a80-b3d9-4c267606ceee","uuid":"fe996965-754f-4bcc-8eca-1bfae534cf2d"}
